[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: srv1
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Campus DNS resolver service (BIND/named) on srv1 (10.5.0.4), which all clients (including PC1) use for DNS.

Mechanism: PC1’s “slow internet” is due to DNS lookups being refused. Applications wait/retry/fall back (e.g., alternate resolvers/DoH or cached IPs), so pages “hang” and then partially load once some names resolve. Underlying IP transport is healthy, so throughput appears fine once an address is known.

Localization: 
- Client side: PC1 resolv.conf -> “nameserver 10.5.0.4” (confirmed).
- Server side: srv1 is running named and listening on 10.5.0.4:53 (ps/ss show named active on UDP/TCP 53), but it is configured as authoritative-only with recursion disabled. From PC1: “nslookup example.com 10.5.0.4” returns “REFUSED” for example.com and google.com (confirmed). On srv1, /etc/bind/named.conf sets “recursion no;” and only serves zone “jeremysitlab.com” (confirmed).

Not a WAN issue: Latency and throughput are normal (pc1↔r1 iperf ≈60 Mb/s, pc1↔ISP ≈45–50 Mb/s; pings low RTT, no drops). STP/links show no errors or congestion.

Root cause: srv1’s BIND is misconfigured as non-recursive authoritative only, so it refuses client queries for public domains while clients rely on it for DNS.

Corrective fix: Enable recursion on srv1 and provide appropriate upstream resolution (either allow full recursion with root hints if reachable, or configure forwarders to valid upstream resolvers). Example in /etc/bind/named.conf: set “recursion yes;” and add forwarders { <reachable DNS IPs>; }; then reload named. Alternatively, repoint clients’ resolv.conf to a working recursive resolver. Verify by getent/nslookup from PC1. === END ===